Why this only surfaced now, and what it says about the test.

The macOS job had never run the whole suite — it ran filtered steps, and
nothing they named touched these fixtures. The first complete run put seven of
them on a machine where a copied system binary does not execute.

Proof, outside the test entirely:

  cp /usr/bin/yes ./faux-ollama && ./faux-ollama   ->  killed, code 137
  codesign -f -s - ./faux-ollama && ./faux-ollama  ->  alive

The failure mode is worth keeping in mind for the reviewer:
emergency_capacity_reap_failure_is_recoverable_without_a_slot fills all 128
emergency slots, then asserts publish returns EmergencyCapacity. It returned
Identity — publish_inner revalidates identity first (process.rs:143), getpgid
came back -1 on the dead child, and it never reached the capacity branch. The
test was asserting on an error it could not have produced. Now that the child
survives, the branch is genuinely exercised for the first time.

The same shape sits in the other fixtures: they copy /usr/bin/true, whose exit
code they do not check, so a SIGKILLed child would have read as success. Going
through install_system_binary closes that off for all of them at once.

Whole Rust suite on macOS: 3668 passed, 0 failed, 3 ignored, 99.7s — the first
time it has completed there.
